---
// The nav bar every page carries, so that no page is a dead end.
//
// This file exists because the hand-written site had FORTY-FOUR of these and
// no two were the same: measured, 44 pages and 44 distinct nav blocks. They
// differed by exactly three mechanical things, and nothing else:
//
//   1. `class="here"` on the entry for the page you are reading;
//   2. how many `../` the links needed to climb;
//   3. the link to the other edition, which points at this same page.
//
// Only the first and third are left. The second stopped existing when the links
// became absolute, and that is worth more than the lines it saved: with relative
// links the site had TWO different depths to keep straight, one for the
// stylesheet and one for the nav, because `/it/` is one directory down from the
// root and at the top of its own edition at the same time. An absolute path has
// no depth to get wrong.

interface Props {
  /** The URL this page answers at, always absolute, always ending in `/`. */
  url: string;
  /** Which nav entry is the page you are reading. `errors` is `docs`. */
  current?: string;
}

const { url, current } = Astro.props;

const lang = url.startsWith('/it/') || url === '/it/' ? 'it' : 'en';
const root = lang === 'it' ? '/it' : '';

// The other edition's copy of this same page. The 404 page has no copy: it is
// served at whatever address was not found, and `/it/404/` is itself an
// address that does not exist, so its language link goes to the other
// edition's home (a dead link on the one page about dead links, found by the
// devex seat, 2026-09-09).
const other =
  url === '/404/' ? '/it/' : url === '/it/404/' ? '/' : lang === 'en' ? `/it${url}` : url.slice(3);

// Eight entries, each answering a question a visitor actually has.
//
// **The order is the author's, set 2026-09-08, and it reverses the first four
// deliberately.** The row read why · examples · guide · start, which is the
// order somebody ASKS: why should I care, show me the code, teach it to me, how
// do I run it. It now reads start · guide · examples · why, which is the order
// somebody ACTS: a visitor who has decided to try this should meet the way in
// first and the argument for it last. The old reasoning is kept here rather
// than deleted, because it was not wrong, it was answering a different
// question: a nav sorted by curiosity serves a reader, and one sorted by
// intent serves a user, and this site now has a repository behind it that a
// reader can actually go and use.
//
// The back half is unchanged: project · log · about · thanks. Project stands
// before Log by author instruction (2026-09-08 as well): the log is the
// project's log, so the thing comes before its diary. The eighth, Thanks, was
// seven's displaced page until the author put it back (2026-09-07): the credits
// are the one page whose subject is other people, and a nav that hides them
// reads as a project that forgot them.
//
// It was eight, and the eight were the project's own shelves rather than the
// visitor's: Docs, Zen, Why, Self-hosted, Log, Panel, Thanks, Author. Three of
// those are one page now (`/project/`) and two are another (`/about/`), by
// author instruction 2026-09-06, which is what made room for Examples and
// Start without the row growing. Every surveyed language site has a learning
// entry and an install entry in its top nav; this one had neither.
//
// **The second entry says `Docs` in both editions since 2026-11-27**, where it
// said `Guide` and `Guida`: the author's question that morning, meant as
// *perhaps the Guide entry should be renamed Docs?*, answered yes by the
// coordinator as the batch's default the author may turn. It was the one label
// that matched neither its address, `/docs/`, nor its page's title,
// *Documentation*. The Italian is `Docs` and not `Documentazione` on the
// measurement, the way `Grazie` was chosen over `Ringraziamenti`: with the
// longer word the Italian row took two lines from 980px and THREE between 850
// and 801px, where `Docs` keeps it on one line down to 910px (it was 920px
// with `Guida`) and scrolls sideways only from 590px (600px with `Guida`).
// The English row is 8px narrower than with `Guide` and breaks where it did.
//
// **Rebel asked for an entry the same morning, and has none yet**: the author's
// words, meant as *Rebel has no menu entry*. Measured that day, Rebel after
// Spec made the row 579px in English and 666px in Italian at 1200px (520 and
// 607 without it): the English row went to two lines from 870px where it held
// one down to 830px, and the Italian to two lines from 960px and three between
// 830 and 801px, the state § Style guide's rule forbids. A tenth entry fits
// only by displacing one, and which one is the author's choice, put to them
// with those widths; until then Rebel is reached from the home page and the
// examples. The widths, every 10px from 1200 to 390 in both editions, are
// site/README.md's, beside Spec's.
//
// **Rebel took Log's place the same evening, by the author's choice** (their
// answer 2a, meant as: *Rebel replaces Log; the log is the project's diary and
// lives under Project*). The log is one click below Project now, linked from
// its lede and after its chain, wearing a crumb that names Project, which is
// the entry it marks. Rebel stands after Spec and not where Log stood: Log was
// one of the project's own shelves, Why, Project and its diary, while Rebel
// is something a visitor takes, so it continues the intent order of the front
// half, run it, learn it, see it, take the language, take the library. Five
// letters where Log had three widened the English row by 16px, and the row
// held where it did only with its gaps a little tighter (`style.css`, the
// nav): measured that evening, every 10px and then every pixel at the edges,
// the English row holds one line from 815px where it held from 821px, and
// scrolls sideways from 500px where it did from 505px; the Italian, narrower
// with `Rebel` than with `Diario`, from 879px and 564px where it did from
// 909px and 593px.
const entries = [
  { key: 'start', slug: 'start', en: 'Start', it: 'Inizia' },
  { key: 'docs', slug: 'docs', en: 'Docs', it: 'Docs' },
  { key: 'examples', slug: 'examples', en: 'Examples', it: 'Esempi' },
  // **The ninth, added 2026-09-11 by author instruction** — *maybe it makes
  // sense to put spec in the top menu, because it has become very important* —
  // and it is the one entry that breaks § Style guide's rule that a page enters
  // this row only by displacing another. It displaces nothing, and the reason is
  // measured rather than argued: the README's own figures are a NINE-item row at
  // 517px in English and 540px in Italian, both reaching the sideways scroll
  // only at 390px, so nine is a width this row has already carried. It sits
  // after Examples because the intent order runs run it, learn it, see it, take
  // it: the specification is what a visitor pastes into a prompt, and the file
  // it points at is the one artifact the whole site is about.
  { key: 'spec', slug: 'spec', en: 'Spec', it: 'Specifica' },
  { key: 'rebel', slug: 'rebel', en: 'Rebel', it: 'Rebel' },
  { key: 'why', slug: 'why', en: 'Why', it: 'Perché' },
  { key: 'project', slug: 'project', en: 'Project', it: 'Progetto' },
  { key: 'about', slug: 'about', en: 'Author', it: 'Autore' },
  // `Grazie`, the page's own title, and not `Ringraziamenti`: the longer word
  // put the Italian row on two lines at 900px where the English held one.
  { key: 'thanks', slug: 'about/thanks', en: 'Thanks', it: 'Grazie' },
];
---
<nav class="site">
  <div>
    <a class="mark" href={`${root}/`}>
      <svg viewBox="0 0 24 32" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="var(--red)" d="M 14 0 L2 18 h8 L7 32 L22 12 h-9 z"/></svg>
      <span>Heroes</span>
    </a>
    <ul>
      {entries.map((e) => (
        <li><a class={e.key === current ? 'here' : undefined} href={`${root}/${e.slug}/`}>{lang === 'it' ? e.it : e.en}</a></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    {lang === 'en'
      ? <a class="lang" href={other} hreflang="it" lang="it" title="Questa pagina in italiano">IT</a>
      : <a class="lang" href={other} hreflang="en" lang="en" title="This page in English">EN</a>}
    <!-- **The repository opened, and the repair was what this comment said it
         would be: deleting the word.** The caveat below did its job for the
         days it was needed and is kept because it records why the link was
         never repointed, which is the decision that made today cost nothing.
         The `title` now says what is on the other side instead of when it will
         be reachable.

         The address stays, and the warning becomes visible.
         `site/README.md` § Launch order records the author's decision that
         these links stay clickable: the addresses are final, so a live link is
         a door that is not open rather than a wrong address, and the day the
         repository opens there is nothing to put back. What the marketing seat
         measured is the other half of it: the only warning here was a `title`,
         which does not exist on a phone and which nobody hovers before clicking
         a link they mean to click, so 171 of 180 pages carried this with no
         visible caveat at all. The word in the link says it now, and the day
         the repository opens the repair is deleting two words. Repointing the
         link is the seat's own proposal and it is the author's to take, because
         it is their decision it would reverse.

         The word was `soon` and `presto`, which is a DURATION claim, and
         site/README.md forbids those outright: a duration is a boast and this
         one had no date behind it. Every other caveat on this site states a
         CONDITION instead, "it opens with the code", which cannot go stale. So
         does this one now. Found by the marketing seat. -->
    <!-- `target` and `rel` are written here rather than added by
         `fillOutbound` in `src/lib/figures.ts`, which is where every other
         outbound link on the site gets them: the nav is a component and the
         byline is a partial, so neither passes through `page()`. Two places by
         hand, so that the rule itself keeps its no exceptions. -->
    <a class="out" target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://github.com/heroes-lang/heroes"
       title={lang === 'it'
         ? 'Il codice del compilatore, la specifica e tutti i registri del progetto'
         : 'The compiler, the specification and every record the project keeps'}>GitHub</a>
  </div>
</nav>
